== 2. the 45-line hand-rolled scanner: the "exact replacement" claim holds ==

`stripBacktickSpans` claims to be "the exact replacement performed by /(`+)[^`\n]*?\1/g,
without its quadratic backtracking". A hand-rolled scanner claiming exact regex equivalence is
the highest-risk line in this diff, so I differential-tested the shipped text (extracted
verbatim from evidence.ts) against the regex it replaces:

  input family                                                      comparisons   mismatches
  every line of all 57 real briefs                                       19,244            0
  exhaustive over {`, ``, ```, a, "x ", "} to depth 6                     55,986            0
  40k+20k random backtick-dense strings                                  60,001            0
  exhaustive backtick-run patterns: 1..4 runs of length 1..7,            204,204            0
  gaps of ""/"a"/"ab", with and without leading/trailing letters
  TOTAL                                                                 339,435            0

Zero divergence. The algorithm is also correct by argument: a full opening run can only close
at the next run (the body cannot contain a backtick), so `next.length >= openingLength` is the
greedy case, and when it fails the capture can only backtrack to ticks inside the opening run
itself, i.e. floor(openingLength/2) — which is what the code does, leaving an odd leftover tick
to pair with the next run on the following iteration. The comment says this correctly.

Timing, measured here:

  shape                                        old regex        scanner
  "a" + 10,000 backticks + " Behaviour 99"        0.0ms           0.0ms
  "a" + 150k backticks + 150k "x" + citation    584.6ms           0.3ms

Note for the record: the round's report attributes 594.7ms to "a 10k-backtick line". It is not
that shape — 10k backticks is fast under the old regex too. The real quadratic shape is the
150k+150k one, which is the second fixture in the test, and it reproduces at 584.6ms. The
fixture set is right; only the attribution in the report is off.

